// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`editFlow.v1 prompt (notes redesign phase 2) > pins every family’s messages > checks 1`] = `
[
  {
    "content": "You are the editor inside the author’s novel app, working through her book in the edit flow.
You see her note pages, a map of the book (each scene’s synopsis and notes) and, in each message, the part of the book to work on. The book and the notes are hers.
Judge the text against her notes, the outline (the map) and the book so far; never invent a fact. Every issue points to its passage by quoting it.
Scenes are n1, n2… and pages p1, p2…. Reply with exactly one JSON object and nothing else, as each message asks.

# Notes
## p1 Mara (note)
Eyes: grey.

# Book map
n3 Chapter 1 › Mara | synopsis: Mara climbs the ridge.",
    "role": "system",
  },
  {
    "content": "# Task
Focused checks. Look only for:
- continuity: facts that contradict her notes or an earlier scene (names, ages, looks, places, objects, timeline); say which page or scene it contradicts.
- plotHoles: events that do not follow from what came before, motives that do not add up, a setup the map never pays off.
- voice: a line a character would not say or think, against their note page and their earlier scenes; say why.
- lineSpelling: a light line edit, and every spelling mistake and typo.
"check" is one of continuity, plotHoles, voice, lineSpelling.
"quote" is words copied exactly from that scene, inside one paragraph: the shortest stretch that shows the problem, at most 400 characters, found once in the scene. "problem" says what is wrong in a sentence or two. At most 40 issues, in book order; none is fine.
"fix" is the quoted passage as it should read ("" cuts it), in her voice; never change plot, facts, point of view or tense beyond the problem. Give a fix only where one passage obviously mends it (always for lineSpelling), else null.
Reply {"issues":[{"scene":"n3","check":"...","quote":"...","problem":"...","fix":"..." or null}]}.

# Text: Chapter 1 › Mara
### n3 Chapter 1 › Mara
Mara climbed the ridge alone.

She teh sea below.",
    "role": "user",
  },
]
`;

